        "title": "1. Rank of a Matrix & Normal (Canonical) Form",
        "content": "### 1. In-Depth Mathematical Foundations of Matrix Rank\n\n> 🟢 **Rigorous Linear Algebra Definition:** Let $A$ be an $m \\times n$ matrix with entries in $\\mathbb{R}$ (or $\\mathbb{C}$). The **Rank** of $A$, denoted by $\\rho(A)$ or $\\text{rank}(A)$, is defined as the dimension of the vector space spanned by its row vectors (Row Rank) or column vectors (Column Rank). By the Fundamental Rank Theorem of Linear Algebra:\n> $$\\mathbf{\\text{Row Rank}(A) = \\text{Column Rank}(A) = \\rho(A) \\le \\min(m, n)}$$\n\n#### Minor-Based Criterion of Rank:\nA matrix $A$ is said to be of rank $r$ if and only if:\n1. There exists **at least one minor** (determinant of a square submatrix) of order $r$ that is strictly non-zero: $|M_r| \\ne 0$.\n2. **Every minor** of order $(r + 1)$ and higher vanishes identically: $|M_{r+1}| = 0$.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive: Row Echelon Form Reduction\n\n#### Strict Mathematical Criteria for Row Echelon Form:\nA matrix $E$ is in **Row Echelon Form** if:\n1. **Zero Rows at Bottom:** All rows consisting entirely of zeros are grouped at the very bottom.\n2. **Leading Entry (Pivot):** The leading coefficient (the first non-zero entry from the left) of each non-zero row is strictly to the right of the leading coefficient of the row directly above it.\n3. **Zeros Below Pivots:** All entries in a column directly below a pivot entry are zero.\n\n$$\\mathbf{\\rho(A) = \\text{Total Number of Non-Zero Rows in Row Echelon Form}}$$\n\n#### Invariance of Rank under Elementary Row Transformations:\nElementary row transformations do not alter the linear span of the row vectors:\n1. Interchanging two rows: $R_i \\leftrightarrow R_j$ (Permutation matrix $P$, $|P| = -1 \\ne 0$).\n2. Multiplying a row by a non-zero scalar: $R_i \\to k R_i, \\; k \\ne 0$ (Diagonal scaling matrix $D$, $|D| = k \\ne 0$).\n3. Adding a scalar multiple of one row to another: $R_i \\to R_i + c R_j$ (Shear matrix $S$, $|S| = 1 \\ne 0$).\nBecause all elementary matrices are non-singular ($|E| \\ne 0$), rank is strictly invariant: $\\rho(E A) = \\rho(A)$.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Deep-Dive: Normal (Canonical) Form $[I_r\\ 0; 0\\ 0]$\n\nBy using **both elementary row AND elementary column operations**, any arbitrary $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ can be brought into its unique Canonical Normal Form:\n\n$$\\mathbf{N = \\begin{bmatrix} I_r & 0_{r \\times (n-r)} \\\\ 0_{(m-r) \\times r} & 0_{(m-r) \\times (n-r)} \\end{bmatrix}}$$\n\n#### Existence of Non-Singular Matrices $P$ and $Q$:\nThere exist non-singular square matrices $P$ (of order $m$) and $Q$ (of order $n$) representing the product of all row operations and column operations respectively, such that:\n$$\\mathbf{P \\cdot A \\cdot Q = \\begin{bmatrix} I_r & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}}$$\n- **Procedure to compute $P$ and $Q$ simultaneously:**\n  1. Write $A_{m \\times n} = I_m \\cdot A \\cdot I_n$.\n  2. Perform elementary row operations on $A$ and simultaneously on the pre-factor $I_m$.\n  3. Perform elementary column operations on $A$ and simultaneously on the post-factor $I_n$.\n  4. When the central matrix reduces to $[I_r\\ 0; 0\\ 0]$, the left matrix becomes $P$ and the right matrix becomes $Q$!\n\n---\n\n### 4. Fundamental Properties & Sylvester's Rank Inequalities\n\n1. **Sub-multiplicative Property:** $\\rho(A B) \\le \\min(\\rho(A), \\rho(B))$.\n2. **Sylvester's Law of Nullity:** For an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ and $n \\times p$ matrix $B$:\n   $$\\mathbf{\\rho(A) + \\rho(B) - n \\le \\rho(A B) \\le \\min(\\rho(A), \\rho(B))}$$\n3. **Rank of Transpose & Conjugate:** $\\rho(A^T) = \\rho(A)$ and $\\rho(A^T A) = \\rho(A A^T) = \\rho(A)$.\n4. **Rank of Sum:** $\\rho(A + B) \\le \\rho(A) + \\rho(B)$.\n\n---\n\n**Problem:**\nFind the rank of matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Compute the Determinant of $A$**\n$$|A| = (2)(6) - (4)(3) = 12 - 12 = 0$$\nSince $|A| = 0$, the rank cannot be 2: $\\rho(A) < 2$.\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Check Order-1 Minors (Single Entries)**\nThe matrix contains non-zero entries (e.g., $a_{11} = 2 \\ne 0$). Therefore, there is at least one non-vanishing minor of order 1.\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Row Echelon Verification ($R_2 \\to R_2 - 1.5R_1$)**\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\end{bmatrix} \\sim \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$\nThere is exactly **1 non-zero row**.\n\n🎯 Rank of $A$: $\\mathbf{\\rho(A) = 1}$.\n\n---\n\n**University Question (4 Marks):**\nReduce the matrix $A$ to Row Echelon form and determine its rank:\n$$A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 10 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Eliminate entries below pivot in Column 1**\nApply row operations $R_2 \\to R_2 - 2R_1$ and $R_3 \\to R_3 - 3R_1$:\n$$A \\sim \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Eliminate entry below pivot in Column 3**\nApply $R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2$:\n$$A \\sim \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Count Non-Zero Rows**\nThe echelon form contains 2 non-zero rows ($[1, 2, 3]$ and $[0, 0, 1]$) and 1 zero row.\n\n🎯 Row Echelon Rank: $\\mathbf{\\rho(A) = 2}$.\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - MMDU Annual Exam):**\nReduce matrix $A$ to its Normal Form $\\begin{bmatrix} I_r & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ using elementary row AND column transformations, and find non-singular matrices $P$ and $Q$ such that $PAQ = \\begin{bmatrix} I_r & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$:\n$$A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Setup Identity Matrices $A = I_3 A I_3$**\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} A \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Elementary Row Operation $R_2 \\to R_2 - R_1$**\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} A I_3$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Elementary Column Operations $C_2 \\to C_2 - C_1, \\; C_3 \\to C_3 - 2C_1$**\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} A \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n**Step 4 🏁: Row Operation $R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2$ and Column Operation $C_3 \\to C_3 - C_2$**\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} A \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\nThis is the standard Normal Form $\\begin{bmatrix} I_2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Canonical Normal Form: $\\begin{bmatrix} I_2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ | Rank $\\mathbf{\\rho(A) = 2}$ | $P = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, $Q = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n</div>"
