        "sectionId": "m1-u1-s3",
        "title": "3. Consistency of System of Linear Equations AX = B",
        "content": "### 1. Comprehensive Theory: System of Linear Equations $AX = B$\n\nA system of $m$ linear equations in $n$ unknowns $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n$ is represented in matrix notation as:\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} a_{11} & a_{12} & \\dots & a_{1n} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22} & \\dots & a_{2n} \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\ a_{m1} & a_{m2} & \\dots & a_{mn} \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\\\ x_2 \\\\ \\vdots \\\\ x_n \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\\\ b_2 \\\\ \\vdots \\\\ b_m \\end{bmatrix} \\iff \\mathbf{A X = B}$$\n\n#### The Rouché–Capelli Theorem:\n> 🟢 **Theorem Statement:** A system of linear equations $A X = B$ is **consistent** (possesses at least one solution) if and only if the rank of the coefficient matrix $A$ equals the rank of the augmented matrix $[A \\mid B]$:\n> $$\\mathbf{\\rho(A) = \\rho([A \\mid B])}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Complete Classification of Solution Regimes\n\n```\n                                  [ System AX = B ]\n                                          |\n                 +------------------------+------------------------+\n                 |                                                 |\n         rho(A) != rho([A|B])                              rho(A) == rho([A|B])\n                 |                                                 |\n         [ INCONSISTENT ]                                   [ CONSISTENT ]\n         Zero Solutions!                                           |\n         (Hyperplanes parallel                             +-------+-------+\n          or non-intersecting)                             |               |\n                                                      rho(A) == n     rho(A) = r < n\n                                                           |               |\n                                                   [ UNIQUE SOLN ]  [ INFINITE SOLNS ]\n                                                   Single point     (n - r) free parameters\n                                                   intersection     Forms an affine subspace\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Homogeneous Linear Systems $AX = 0$\n\nFor $AX = 0$, $B = 0$. Since augmenting a column of zeros cannot increase matrix rank, $\\rho(A) = \\rho([A \\mid 0])$ always holds. Therefore, **a homogeneous system is ALWAYS consistent** and always possesses at least the trivial solution $X = 0$.\n\n#### Nature of Solutions for Square Systems ($n \\times n$):\n1. **Trivial Solution ($X = 0$):**\n   - Occurs when $\\rho(A) = n \\iff |A| \\ne 0$ (Non-singular matrix).\n   - The columns of $A$ are linearly independent; the only solution is $x_1 = x_2 = \\dots = x_n = 0$.\n2. **Non-Trivial Solutions (Infinite Non-Zero Solutions):**\n   - Occurs when $\\rho(A) = r < n \\iff |A| = 0$ (Singular matrix), yielding $(n - r)$ linearly independent parameters.\n\n> 🔴 **Examiner's Trap:** When an exam problem asks: *\"Find values of $k$ for which the homogeneous equations have non-trivial (non-zero) solutions\"*, simply set the determinant to zero: $\\mathbf{|A| = 0}$!\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - Classic MMDU Exam Problem):**\nInvestigate for what values of $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ the system of equations:\n$$\\begin{cases} x + y + z = 6 \\\\ x + 2y + 3z = 10 \\\\ x + 2y + \\lambda z = \\mu \\end{cases}$$\nhas: (i) No solution, (ii) A unique solution, (iii) Infinitely many solutions.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Setup Augmented Matrix and Echelon Reduction**\n$$[A \\mid B] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & \\lambda & \\mu \\end{array} \\right] \\xrightarrow{\\substack{R_2 \\to R_2 - R_1 \\\\ R_3 \\to R_3 - R_1}} \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\lambda - 1 & \\mu - 6 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n$$\\xrightarrow{R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2} \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda - 3 & \\mu - 10 \\end{array} \\right]$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Case Analysis by Rouché–Capelli Theorem**\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{1. Case (i) No Solution (Inconsistent): } & \\lambda = 3, \\; \\mu \\ne 10 \\implies \\rho(A) = 2 \\ne \\rho([A \\mid B]) = 3 \\\\\n\\text{2. Case (ii) Unique Solution: } & \\lambda \\ne 3, \\; \\forall \\mu \\implies \\rho(A) = \\rho([A \\mid B]) = 3 = n \\\\\n\\text{3. Case (iii) Infinite Solutions: } & \\lambda = 3, \\; \\mu = 10 \\implies \\rho(A) = \\rho([A \\mid B]) = 2 < n \\; (z = k)\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 **Verified Result:** (i) No solution for $\\lambda = 3, \\mu \\ne 10$ | (ii) Unique for $\\lambda \\ne 3$ | (iii) Infinite for $\\lambda = 3, \\mu = 10$.\n</div>"
