        "sectionId": "m1-u2-s2",
        "title": "2. Curvature, Centre & Chord of Curvature (Cartesian, Parametric, Polar)",
        "content": "### 1. Curvature, Radius & Center of Curvature: Comprehensive Theory\n\n#### Physical Concept of Curvature ($\\kappa$):\n> 🟢 **Definition:** Let $P$ and $Q$ be two neighboring points on a smooth curve separated by arc length $\\Delta s$. If the tangent vectors at $P$ and $Q$ make angles $\\psi$ and $\\psi + \\Delta\\psi$ with the positive $x$-axis, the **Curvature** $\\kappa$ is the rate of turning of the tangent line per unit arc length:\n> $$\\mathbf{\\kappa = \\lim_{\\Delta s \\to 0} \\left| \\frac{\\Delta\\psi}{\\Delta s} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{d\\psi}{ds} \\right|}$$\n\n#### Radius of Curvature ($\\rho$):\nThe Radius of Curvature $\\rho$ is the reciprocal of the curvature:\n$$\\mathbf{\\rho = \\frac{1}{\\kappa} = \\left| \\frac{ds}{d\\psi} \\right|}$$\n- A straight line has zero bending $\\implies \\kappa = 0 \\implies \\rho = \\infty$.\n- A circle of radius $R$ bends uniformly $\\implies \\kappa = 1/R \\implies \\rho = R$.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Analytical Formulas for Radius of Curvature $\\rho$\n\n| Coordinate Representation | Curve Equation | Radius of Curvature Formula $\\rho$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cartesian $y = f(x)$** | $y = f(x)$ | $\\mathbf{\\rho = \\frac{\\left(1 + y_1^2\\right)^{3/2}}{y_2}} \\quad \\left(y_1 = \\frac{dy}{dx}, \\; y_2 = \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\right)$ |\n| **Cartesian $x = g(y)$** | $x = g(y)$ | $\\mathbf{\\rho = \\frac{\\left(1 + x_1^2\\right)^{3/2}}{x_2}} \\quad \\left(x_1 = \\frac{dx}{dy}, \\; x_2 = \\frac{d^2x}{dy^2}\\right)$ |\n| **Parametric** | $x = x(t), \\; y = y(t)$ | $\\mathbf{\\rho = \\frac{\\left(x'^2 + y'^2\\right)^{3/2}}{x' y'' - y' x''}}$ |\n| **Polar Form** | $r = f(\\theta)$ | $\\mathbf{\\rho = \\frac{\\left(r^2 + r_1^2\\right)^{3/2}}{r^2 + 2r_1^2 - r r_2}} \\quad \\left(r_1 = \\frac{dr}{d\\theta}, \\; r_2 = \\frac{d^2r}{d\\theta^2}\\right)$ |\n| **Pedal Form ($p$-$r$)** | $p = f(r)$ | $\\mathbf{\\rho = r \\frac{dr}{dp}}$ |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Center of Curvature $(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y})$ and Chord of Curvature\n\nThe circle that shares the same tangent and curvature as the curve at point $P(x, y)$ is the **Osculating Circle (Circle of Curvature)**. Its center $C(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y})$ is given by:\n$$\\mathbf{\\bar{x} = x - \\frac{y_1(1 + y_1^2)}{y_2}}, \\quad \\mathbf{\\bar{y} = y + \\frac{1 + y_1^2}{y_2}}$$\n- **Equation of Circle of Curvature:** $(x - \\bar{x})^2 + (y - \\bar{y})^2 = \\rho^2$.\n- **Evolute:** The locus of the centers of curvature of all points on a curve is called the **Evolute** of that curve.\n\n#### Chords of Curvature:\n- **Chord parallel to $x$-axis:** $C_x = 2 \\rho \\sin\\psi = \\mathbf{\\frac{2 y_1 (1 + y_1^2)}{y_2}}$\n- **Chord parallel to $y$-axis:** $C_y = 2 \\rho \\cos\\psi = \\mathbf{\\frac{2 (1 + y_1^2)}{y_2}}$\n- **Chord through the Pole (Polar Curve $r = f(\\theta)$):** $C_p = 2 \\rho \\sin\\phi = \\mathbf{\\frac{2r(r^2 + r_1^2)}{r^2 + 2r_1^2 - r r_2}}$\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks):**\nFind the radius of curvature at any point $t$ on the cycloid $x = a(t + \\sin t), \\; y = a(1 - \\cos t)$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Compute First and Second Derivatives w.r.t $t$**\n- $x' = \\frac{dx}{dt} = a(1 + \\cos t) = 2a \\cos^2\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)$\n- $y' = \\frac{dy}{dt} = a \\sin t = 2a \\sin\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)$\n- $x'' = -a \\sin t, \\quad y'' = a \\cos t$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Denominator $x' y'' - y' x''$ and Numerator $(x'^2 + y'^2)^{3/2}$**\n$$x' y'' - y' x'' = a^2(1 + \\cos t) = 2a^2 \\cos^2\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)$$\n$$(x'^2 + y'^2)^{3/2} = \\left[4a^2 \\cos^2\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\right]^{3/2} = 8a^3 \\cos^3\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)$$\n$$\\rho = \\frac{8a^3 \\cos^3(t/2)}{2a^2 \\cos^2(t/2)} = 4a \\cos\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Radius of Curvature: $\\mathbf{\\rho = 4a \\cos\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}$.\n</div>\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - MMDU Question Paper):**\nFind the coordinates of the centre of curvature $(\\bar{X}, \\bar{Y})$ and the equation of the evolute of the parabola $y^2 = 4ax$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Derivatives for $y^2 = 4ax$**\n$$2y y_1 = 4a \\implies y_1 = \\frac{2a}{y}, \\quad y_2 = -\\frac{4a^2}{y^3}$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Centre of Curvature Formula**\n$$\\bar{X} = x - \\frac{y_1(1 + y_1^2)}{y_2} = x + \\frac{y^2 + 4a^2}{2a} = 3x + 2a$$\n$$\\bar{Y} = y + \\frac{1 + y_1^2}{y_2} = -\\frac{y^3}{4a^2}$$\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Eliminate $(x, y)$ to Obtain the Evolute Equation**\nSubstitute $x = \\frac{\\bar{X} - 2a}{3}$ and $y^2 = (4a^2 \\bar{Y})^{2/3}$ into $y^2 = 4ax$:\n$$27 a \\bar{Y}^2 = 4 (\\bar{X} - 2a)^3$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Centre of Curvature: $(\\bar{X}, \\bar{Y}) = \\left(3x + 2a, \\, -\\frac{y^3}{4a^2}\\right)$ | Evolute: $\\mathbf{27a Y^2 = 4(X - 2a)^3}$.\n</div>"
