        "title": "2. Euler's Theorem on Homogeneous Functions",
        "content": "### 1. Homogeneous Functions & Euler's Theorem: Rigorous Analysis\n\n> 🟢 **Definition:** A multivariable function $f(x, y)$ is **homogeneous of degree $n$** if for any scalar $t > 0$:\n> $$\\mathbf{f(t x, t y) = t^n f(x, y) \\iff f(x, y) = x^n \\, \\phi\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Euler's Theorem on Homogeneous Functions (First & Second Order)\n\n> 🟢 **First-Order Euler's Theorem:** If $u = f(x, y)$ is a homogeneous function of $x$ and $y$ of degree $n$ with continuous partial derivatives, then:\n> $$\\mathbf{x \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} + y \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = n \\, u}$$\n\n> 🟢 **Second-Order Euler's Formula:**\n> $$\\mathbf{x^2 \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x^2} + 2xy \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x \\partial y} + y^2 \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial y^2} = n(n - 1) u}$$\n\n> 🔴 **Examiner's Composite Function Deduction:** If $z = f(u)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ in $(x, y)$, then:\n> 1. $x u_x + y u_y = n \\frac{f(u)}{f'(u)} = g(u)$\n> 2. $x^2 u_{xx} + 2xy u_{xy} + y^2 u_{yy} = g(u)\\,[g'(u) - 1]$\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - MMDU Question Paper):**\nIf $u = \\sin^{-1}\\left( \\frac{x + 2y + 3z}{\\sqrt{x^8 + y^8 + z^8}} \\right)$, prove that $x u_x + y u_y + z u_z = -3 \\tan u$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Convert to Homogeneous Function $v = \\sin u$**\n$$v = \\sin u = \\frac{x + 2y + 3z}{\\sqrt{x^8 + y^8 + z^8}}$$\nDegree of numerator is $1$; degree of denominator is $\\sqrt{t^8} = t^4$. Thus $v$ is homogeneous of degree $n = 1 - 4 = -3$.\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Apply 3-Variable Euler's Theorem to $v$**\n$$x \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} + y \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} + z \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} = -3 v$$\nUsing $\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} = \\cos u \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}$:\n$$\\cos u \\left(x u_x + y u_y + z u_z\\right) = -3 \\sin u \\implies x u_x + y u_y + z u_z = -3 \\tan u$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Result Proved: $\\mathbf{x \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} + y \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} + z \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} = -3\\tan u}$.\n</div>\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - MMDU Previous Paper):**\nIf $u = \\tan^{-1}\\left( \\frac{x^3 + y^3}{x - y} \\right)$, prove that $x^2 u_{xx} + 2xy u_{xy} + y^2 u_{yy} = \\sin 2u\\,(2\\cos 2u - 1) = \\sin 4u - \\sin 2u$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Define $z = \\tan u$ (Degree $n = 3 - 1 = 2$)**\n$$x z_x + y z_y = 2z \\implies x \\sec^2 u \\, u_x + y \\sec^2 u \\, u_y = 2\\tan u \\implies x u_x + y u_y = \\frac{2\\tan u}{\\sec^2 u} = \\sin 2u = g(u)$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Second-Order Euler Identity**\n$$x^2 u_{xx} + 2xy u_{xy} + y^2 u_{yy} = g(u)\\,[g'(u) - 1] = \\sin 2u\\,(2\\cos 2u - 1) = \\sin 4u - \\sin 2u$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Second-Order Euler Identity Proved: $\\mathbf{x^2 u_{xx} + 2xy u_{xy} + y^2 u_{yy} = \\sin 2u (2\\cos 2u - 1)}$.\n</div>"
