        "content": "### 1. In-Depth Theory of Infinite Series Convergence & Divergence\n\nLet $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty u_n = u_1 + u_2 + \\dots + u_n + \\dots$ be an infinite series with partial sums $S_k = \\sum_{n=1}^k u_n$.\n- **Necessary Condition ($n^{\\text{th}}$ Term Test):** If $\\sum u_n$ converges, then $\\mathbf{\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} u_n = 0}$. If $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} u_n \\ne 0$, the series **strictly diverges**.\n- **Geometric Series $\\sum r^n$:** Converges iff $|r| < 1$.\n- **Harmonic $p$-Series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^p}$:** Converges strictly if $\\mathbf{p > 1}$, and diverges if $\\mathbf{p \\le 1}$.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Systematic Positive-Term Convergence Tests Pipeline\n\n1. **Limit Comparison Test:** If $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{u_n}{v_n} = l$ ($0 < l < \\infty$, finite and non-zero), then $\\sum u_n$ and $\\sum v_n$ converge or diverge together.\n2. **D'Alembert's Ratio Test:** Let $\\mathbf{L = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{u_n}{u_{n+1}}}$:\n   - $\\mathbf{L > 1} \\implies$ **Convergent** | $\\mathbf{L < 1} \\implies$ **Divergent** | $\\mathbf{L = 1} \\implies$ **Test Fails** (use Raabe's or Logarithmic test).\n3. **Raabe's Test (when $L = 1$):** Evaluate $\\mathbf{R = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} n\\left(\\frac{u_n}{u_{n+1}} - 1\\right)}$:\n   - $\\mathbf{R > 1} \\implies$ **Convergent** | $\\mathbf{R < 1} \\implies$ **Divergent** | $\\mathbf{R = 1} \\implies$ **Test Fails**.\n4. **Logarithmic Test (when $L = 1$ with exponents/$e$):** Evaluate $\\mathbf{L' = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} n \\ln\\left(\\frac{u_n}{u_{n+1}}\\right)}$:\n   - $\\mathbf{L' > 1} \\implies$ **Convergent** | $\\mathbf{L' < 1} \\implies$ **Divergent**.\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - Classic MMDU Exam Problem):**\nExamine the convergence of the series:\n$$\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 4} + \\frac{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6} + \\dots + \\frac{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\dots (2n-1)}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\dots (2n)} + \\dots$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Ratio Test Setup**\nHere $\\frac{u_n}{u_{n+1}} = \\frac{2n+2}{2n+1} \\implies L = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{2n+2}{2n+1} = 1 \\implies$ **Ratio Test Fails!**\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Apply Raabe's Test**\n$$R = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} n \\left( \\frac{2n+2}{2n+1} - 1 \\right) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n}{2n+1} = \\frac{1}{2} < 1$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Ratio test fails ($L=1$); Raabe's Test yields $R = \\frac{1}{2} < 1 \\implies$ Series is **Divergent**.\n</div>"
