        "title": "2. Cauchy's Root Test, Alternating Series & Leibnitz's Rule",
        "content": "### 1. Cauchy's Root Test, Alternating Series & Leibnitz's Rule\n\n#### A. Cauchy's $n^{\\text{th}}$ Root Test:\n> 🟢 **Theorem:** Let $\\sum u_n$ be a positive-term series and evaluate $\\mathbf{L = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} (u_n)^{1/n}}$:\n> - $\\mathbf{L < 1} \\implies$ **Convergent** | $\\mathbf{L > 1} \\implies$ **Divergent** | $\\mathbf{L = 1} \\implies$ **Test Fails**.\n\n#### B. Alternating Series & Leibnitz's Rule:\n> 🟢 **Theorem:** An alternating series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (-1)^{n-1} u_n = u_1 - u_2 + u_3 - u_4 + \\dots$ ($u_n > 0$) converges if:\n> 1. **Monotonic Decrease:** $u_{n+1} \\le u_n$ for all $n$.\n> 2. **Vanishing Limit:** $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} u_n = 0$.\n\n#### C. Absolute vs Conditional Convergence:\n- **Absolutely Convergent:** $\\sum |u_n|$ converges (e.g., $\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n^2}$).\n- **Conditionally Convergent:** $\\sum u_n$ converges via Leibnitz's test, but $\\sum |u_n|$ diverges (e.g., $\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n}$ and $\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{\\sqrt{n}}$).\n\n---\n\n**University Question (4 Marks - MMDU Question Paper):**\nTest the alternating series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{\\sqrt{n}}$ for absolute and conditional convergence.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Verify Leibnitz's Conditions**\nHere $u_n = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} > 0$. Since $\\sqrt{n+1} > \\sqrt{n} \\implies u_{n+1} < u_n$, and $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0$, the series **converges** by Leibnitz's Rule.\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Check Absolute Series $\\sum |u_n|$**\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty |u_n| = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^{1/2}}$$\nSince $p = \\frac{1}{2} \\le 1$, $\\sum |u_n|$ **diverges**. Therefore, the series is **Conditionally Convergent**.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 $\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is **Conditionally Convergent** | $\\sum \\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n^2}$ is **Absolutely Convergent**.\n</div>"
