        "title": "3. Fourier Series: Euler's Formulae & Discontinuity",
        "content": "### 1. Fourier Series: Foundations & Euler's Formulas\n\n#### Fourier Representation in $(-\\pi, \\pi)$ or $(0, 2\\pi)$:\n$$\\mathbf{f(x) = \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left[ a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx) \\right]}$$\n$$\\mathbf{a_0 = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi f(x) \\, dx, \\quad a_n = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi f(x) \\cos(nx) \\, dx, \\quad b_n = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi f(x) \\sin(nx) \\, dx}$$\n\n#### Dirichlet's Conditions & Jump Discontinuity:\nAt any finite jump discontinuity $x = c$, the Fourier series converges to the average of the left and right limits:\n$$\\mathbf{S(c) = \\frac{f(c^+) + f(c^-)}{2}}$$\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - MMDU Question Paper):**\nObtain the Fourier series for $f(x) = x^2$ in $(-\\pi, \\pi)$. Hence deduce $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Symmetry & Coefficients**\nSince $f(-x) = (-x)^2 = x^2 = f(x)$, $f(x)$ is **even** $\\implies b_n = 0$.\n$$a_0 = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_0^\\pi x^2 \\, dx = \\frac{2\\pi^2}{3}, \\quad a_n = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_0^\\pi x^2 \\cos(nx) \\, dx = \\frac{4(-1)^n}{n^2}$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Fourier Expansion & Deduction at $x = \\pi$**\n$$x^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4 \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx) \\implies \\text{At } x = \\pi: \\; \\pi^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} \\implies \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$$\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Fourier Series: $x^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4 \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx)$ | Deduced: $\\mathbf{\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}}$.\n</div>"
