        "content": "### 1. Even/Odd Symmetries, Change of Interval $(-l, l)$ & Half-Range Series\n\n| Parity / Domain | Fourier Coefficients | Series Expansion |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Even Function in $(-l, l)$** | $b_n = 0$, $a_0 = \\frac{2}{l}\\int_0^l f(x)dx$, $a_n = \\frac{2}{l}\\int_0^l f(x)\\cos\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}dx$ | $f(x) = \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}\\right)$ |\n| **Odd Function in $(-l, l)$** | $a_0 = 0, a_n = 0$, $b_n = \\frac{2}{l}\\int_0^l f(x)\\sin\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}dx$ | $f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}\\right)$ |\n| **Half-Range Cosine in $(0, l)$** | $b_n = 0$, $a_0 = \\frac{2}{l}\\int_0^l f(x)dx$, $a_n = \\frac{2}{l}\\int_0^l f(x)\\cos\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}dx$ | Even periodic extension of period $2l$ |\n| **Half-Range Sine in $(0, l)$** | $a_0 = 0, a_n = 0$, $b_n = \\frac{2}{l}\\int_0^l f(x)\\sin\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}dx$ | Odd periodic extension of period $2l$ |\n\n---\n\n**University Question (6 Marks - MMDU Question Paper):**\nFind the Fourier series for the triangular wave $f(x) = l - |x|$ in the arbitrary interval $(-l, l)$. Hence deduce $\\frac{1}{1^2} + \\frac{1}{3^2} + \\frac{1}{5^2} + \\dots = \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Symmetry Check in $(-l, l)$**\nSince $f(-x) = l - |-x| = f(x)$, $f(x)$ is **even** $\\implies b_n = 0$ and $a_0 = \\frac{2}{l} \\int_0^l (l - x) dx = l$.\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Compute $a_n$ and Deduce at $x = 0$**\n$$a_n = \\frac{2}{l} \\int_0^l (l - x) \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}\\right) dx = \\frac{2l}{n^2\\pi^2}[1 - (-1)^n] = \\begin{cases} \\frac{4l}{n^2\\pi^2}, & n \\text{ odd} \\\\ 0, & n \\text{ even} \\end{cases}$$\nAt $x = 0$: $l = \\frac{l}{2} + \\frac{4l}{\\pi^2}\\left(\\frac{1}{1^2} + \\frac{1}{3^2} + \\frac{1}{5^2} + \\dots\\right) \\implies \\sum_{n=1,3,5\\dots}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}$.\n\n<div class=\"example-answer-box\">\n🎯 Fourier Series: $\\mathbf{l - |x| = \\frac{l}{2} + \\frac{4l}{\\pi^2} \\sum_{n=1,3,5\\dots} \\frac{1}{n^2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{l}\\right)}$ | Deduced: $\\mathbf{\\sum_{n=1,3,5\\dots} \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}}$.\n</div>"
